nal early stopping based on validation RMSE with a patience parameter.\\n\\n5) Training objective\\n- Implemented: Regularized MSE objective with L2 penalties on U and V; basic SGD updates with bias terms.\\n- Improvement: Add explicit objective calculation and logging; ensure numerical stability (e.g., clipping predictions to a reasonable range or using a robust loss).\\n\\n6) Initialization and regularization\\n- Implemented: Small random initializations for U and V; L2 penalties applied.\\n- Improvement: Consider more robust initialization (e.g., normalized initialization) and possibly separate regularization weights for U and V.\\n\\n7) Handling unseen users/items\\n- Implemented: Unseen users/items during testing default to the global mean (fallback, no leakage).\\n- Improvement: Optionally implement simple bias terms for users/items or a cold-start strategy that uses global mean plus biases if provided.\\n\\n8) Evaluation\\n- Implemented: RMSE computed on the test/validation set, with a global mean baseline available via fallback. Optional MAE is not implemented but permitted.\\n- Improvement: Print/return both RMSE and MAE; compare against explicitly computed global mean RMSE to quantify baseline gap.\\n\\n9) Baselines\\n- Implemented: Global mean baseline; optional user/item means are not implemented.\\n- Improvement: Add user/item mean baselines and compare; report their RMSE alongside model.\\n\\n10) Reproducibility and determinism\\n- Implemented: Fixed seeds per run and aggregation across seeds; seeds are documented.\\n- Improvement: Also fix Python\'92s random.seed for complete determinism; log seeds and environment (numpy version, pandas version) for full reproducibility.\\n\\n11) Output artifacts\\n- Implemented: Experiment data including seeds and RMSE logs is saved to a NumPy file; no explicit saving of learned embeddings or per-rating predictions.\\n- Improvement: Save user and item factor matrices (U, V) to disk, per-rating predictions for the test set, and a metrics JSON/CSV with epoch-level metrics; provide a concise summary log.\\n\\n12) Experiment management\\n- Implemented: Multiple seeds; aggregation of val RMSE per epoch; saves results file.\\n- Improvement: Support varying K values as well; aggregate mean/std across seeds and K; produce a CSV/JSON summary with mean and std across seeds and K.\\n\\n13) Efficiency and scalability\\n- Implemented: In-memory SGD on 100k ratings; avoids dense user-item matrices; uses per-sample updates.\\n- Improvement: Prefer sparse structures (CSR) and minibatch SGD; verify memory usage on 1M-scale; consider sparse rating matrix R and negative sampling for implicit feedback not required here but can be added for scalability.\\n\\n14) Code structure and dependencies\\n- Implemented: Uses NumPy, Pandas, scikit-learn; Python 3.8+ compatible; functions modular but largely monolithic in a single script.\\n- Improvement: Refactor into modular components: data_loader, model, trainer, evaluator; provide a clean CLI for seeds, K, lr, reg; add unit tests.\\n\\n15) Documentation and reproducibility artifacts\\n- Implemented: No README or run instructions present in the code snippet; requires external docs.\\n- Improvement: Provide a concise README with run commands, input expectations, dependencies, and expected outputs; document seeds and hyperparameters clearly.\\n\\nOverall verdict: 55/100. The approach is a solid baseline but requires several enhancements to satisfy all requirements and improve reproducibility, scalability, and interpretability.', is_satisfactory=False)\
